.TH FERMIX 1 "2026-09-13" "Fermix" "Fermix Manual"
.SH NAME
fermix \- Elixir-native multi-agent AI platform
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fermix
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fermix
is the engine and the command line of the Fermix platform: a background daemon
that runs the agents, the providers, the tools and the memory, and a set of
verbs that configure it, inspect it and talk to it.
.PP
A Fermix installed from a distribution package runs as a systemd user service.
The package owns the unit at
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/user/fermix.service ,
and
.B fermix service
records which home this account's service runs from, enables the unit and
verifies that the daemon answers. Output from the service goes to the journal:
.IP
journalctl \-\-user \-u fermix
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B fermix setup
Run the setup assistant. It opens the browser wizard when a graphical session
is available and the terminal wizard otherwise; \fB\-\-web\fR, \fB\-\-cli\fR
and \fB\-\-terminal\fR choose one explicitly.
.TP
.B fermix auth
Sign in to a provider account, show the current sign-in, or sign out:
\fBlogin\fR, \fBstatus\fR, \fBlogout\fR.
.TP
.B fermix ask
Send one message to the assistant and print the reply.
.TP
.B fermix chat
Hold a conversation with the assistant in the terminal.
.TP
.B fermix run
Run the daemon in the foreground, in this terminal.
.TP
.B fermix sandbox
Inspect and change which directories agents may read and write.
.TP
.B fermix grant
Grant agents access to one directory.
.TP
.B fermix revoke
Withdraw a directory grant.
.TP
.B fermix service
Manage this account's background service:
\fBinstall\fR [\fB\-\-home\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR],
\fBuninstall\fR, \fBstatus\fR and \fBrun\fR, which is the launch entry point
the packaged unit uses. \fB\-\-json\fR prints one machine-readable envelope.
.TP
.B fermix start
Start the installed background service.
.TP
.B fermix stop
Stop the installed background service.
.TP
.B fermix restart
Restart the installed background service.
.TP
.B fermix status
Show the daemon's status and an overview of what it is running.
.TP
.B fermix health
Show the health the daemon evaluates for itself.
.TP
.B fermix voice
Show the local voice companion's status.
.TP
.B fermix acp
Bridge an agent client's standard input and output to the daemon, and forget a
remembered client identity.
.TP
.B fermix browser
Install, remove, or inspect the native-messaging bridge the Fermix browser
extension uses to hand one of your own tabs to Fermix.
.TP
.B fermix browser-bridge
Pump one browser extension connection to the daemon. The browser starts this
through the wrapper the install verb writes; it is not run by hand.
.TP
.B fermix agents
Show the main agent and its workers.
.TP
.B fermix capabilities
Show the capabilities registered in this engine.
.TP
.B fermix skills
List, view and reload the installed skills.
.TP
.B fermix plugins
List the catalog, install, enable, disable and sign in to plugins.
.TP
.B fermix pair
Pair a mobile companion device.
.TP
.B fermix devices
List and revoke paired mobile devices.
.TP
.B fermix settings
Show and change the running service's settings from a terminal, with no
browser: \fBlist\fR (the default) names every section, \fBshow\fR
\fISECTION\fR [\fB\-\-info\fR] prints its keys and values, \fBset\fR
\fISECTION KEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR... changes one or more keys, \fBsecret set\fR
\fIID\fR [\fB\-\-stdin\fR] and \fBsecret clear\fR \fIID\fR store and forget a
secret, \fBprimary\fR [\fIPROVIDER\fR] lists the providers or makes one primary,
and \fBreload\fR reads config.toml again after an edit made outside Fermix.
A secret is never taken as an argument, which would leave it in the process
list and the shell history: \fBsecret set\fR prompts for it without echo, or
reads it from standard input with \fB\-\-stdin\fR. The running daemon checks
and applies every change; one that needs a restart says so, and
\fBfermix restart\fR applies it. \fB\-\-json\fR prints the result as one JSON
object. Exits 3, changing nothing, when no daemon is running. For a
service bound to another home, name it:
\fBFERMIX_HOME=\fR\fIPATH\fR \fBfermix settings\fR.
.TP
.B fermix memory
Review and restore what the assistant remembers.
.TP
.B fermix home
.RI "export " FILE " [--passphrase-file " PATH "] [--mark-moved --to " TEXT "] | import " FILE " --into " ROOT " [--passphrase-file " PATH "] | mark-moved --to " TEXT " [--json] | status [--json] | snapshots status|run [--json] | snapshots passphrase --passphrase-file " PATH " [--json] | enroll-code [--json] | enroll --code-file " PATH " [--snapshot-bucket " BUCKET " --snapshot-region " REGION "] | candidates [--json] | move --to " ID " [--recovery-passphrase-file " PATH "] [--retire] [--json]"
Carry this home to another host. \fBexport\fR seals everything that makes
the home the primary engine into one file under a passphrase you hold, and
refuses while the engine that owns the home is running. \fBimport\fR
restores an archive into an empty directory as the next primary, one epoch
above the archived home. The passphrase is read from the file or asked for
without echo; whoever holds it can stand up the primary. The old home must be
marked moved before it runs again: \fBmark-moved\fR records where it went, and
from then on it refuses to start as the primary. \fBexport --mark-moved\fR
writes that marker right after the archive is sealed, so a service that
relaunches the engine between two commands cannot start the old home as the
primary. \fBstatus\fR shows the home's role, primary epoch, moved marker and
snapshots, from the running engine or, when it is stopped, from the home's own
files. A primary writes encrypted snapshots of itself to S3 on a cadence once
a recovery passphrase is set with \fBsnapshots passphrase\fR; Fermix keeps
only the key derived from it, never the passphrase. \fBsnapshots run\fR takes
one now, and \fBsnapshots status\fR shows the last stored one. \fBimport\fR
restores a snapshot downloaded from the bucket with the recovery passphrase,
as it restores an archive.
To move the primary to a new server instead, \fBenroll-code\fR opens a
code on the primary, valid once for thirty minutes, and \fBenroll\fR
\fB\-\-code\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR makes a fresh home on the new server a
candidate, with no engine running, and removes the code file.
\fB\-\-snapshot\-bucket\fR and \fB\-\-snapshot\-region\fR name the bucket
the new server keeps its snapshots in. The primary holds the candidate for
24 hours: \fBcandidates\fR lists it, and
\fBmove \-\-to\fR \fIID\fR moves this home to it under a recovery passphrase
read from \fB\-\-recovery\-passphrase\-file\fR or asked twice without echo.
The engine then restarts in the device role and carries the move out, and
\fBstatus\fR shows where it stands. \fB\-\-retire\fR is for a server that is
itself the primary, being replaced by another server and then destroyed: it
hands the home over and then serves nothing, instead of staying a desktop of
the new one.
.TP
.B fermix logs
Show the daemon's own log file.
.TP
.B fermix upgrade
Update the engine. An engine installed from a distribution package refuses and
names the command for that family, because the package manager owns the files.
.TP
.B fermix uninstall
Remove an application-managed installation.
.TP
.B fermix migrate-to-app
Move a Homebrew formula install to the macOS application.
.TP
.B fermix doctor
Run post-install diagnostics and print what to do about each finding.
.TP
.B fermix diagnostics
.RI "export --offline [--json]"
Collect a bounded, redacted support bundle from what this machine can answer
with no daemon running. Every source reports available, unavailable or not
applicable, so a stopped daemon or an unreadable journal is evidence rather
than a lost bundle.
.TP
.B fermix version
Print the version of this engine.
.TP
.B fermix help
Print the command summary.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.fermix
The default Fermix home: configuration, memory, logs and the control socket.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fermix/service.json
Which home this account's background service runs from.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/fermix.service
The service unit the package owns.
.TP
.I /usr/share/fermix/engine.json
The installed engine's identity.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/fermix/cosign
The signature verifier the engine uses for plugins and releases.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR loginctl (1)
